                <h2>Why Account Tier Management Instead of Unified Management</h2>
<p>When teams initially manage only 3-5 accounts, unified management is still manageable. But when the number of accounts expands to 10 or more, with each account serving different products, markets, and campaign stages, a one-size-fits-all approach becomes problematic.</p>
<p>The biggest issue with unified management is that high-performing accounts get dragged down by underperformers, promising accounts don't receive resource priority, and team energy gets consumed by problem accounts. The essence of tier management is to allocate limited operational resources (time, manpower, budget) differentially, allowing efficient accounts to generate higher returns while underperforming accounts get optimized or cut quickly.</p>
<h2>What Are the Core Criteria for Account Tiering</h2>
<p>Account tiering primarily examines three dimensions: historical performance data, current ROI performance, and strategic positioning.</p>
<p>Historical data determines the account's baseline rating. Accounts that have run stably for over 30 days with substantial cumulative conversions naturally have more value than new accounts. Current ROI reflects the account's immediate health; accounts with consistent ROI above team targets should receive more budget support. Strategic positioning determines that some accounts shouldn't be easily abandoned even with poor short-term ROI, such as brand awareness accounts or new product testing accounts.</p>
<p>Common tiering approaches in practice divide accounts into three levels:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Top-tier accounts</strong>: Stable ROI, high spend volume, core contributors to performance</li>
<li><strong>Middle-tier accounts</strong>: Fluctuating ROI, optimization potential, require focused attention</li>
<li><strong>Bottom-tier accounts</strong>: Newly created, testing, or consistently loss-making accounts</li>
</ul>
<h2>How to Accelerate New Account Cold Start</h2>
<p>The cold start phase for new accounts troubles many teams. Without historical data, algorithms need time to learn, and initial costs tend to be higher.</p>
<p>The key during cold start is not saving money, but <strong>accumulating valid data as quickly as possible</strong>. The recommendation is to not overemphasize CPA (cost per acquisition) during the first 3-7 days, but instead focus on letting the system generate at least 50-100 conversion events. Once the system accumulates enough learning samples, evaluate whether to increase budget or pause based on data performance.</p>
<p>If the team operates multiple new accounts simultaneously, adopt a "small budget, multiple testing" strategy: test 3-5 new accounts simultaneously with lower daily budgets, keep the top 1-2 with best data performance for increased investment, and phase out the rest.</p>
<h2>How to Allocate Budget Across Different Tiers</h2>
<p>Budget allocation should follow the "top-tier accounts first" principle, but not simply give 80% of the budget to top-tier accounts.</p>
<p>Top-tier accounts typically consume budget quickly but experience diminishing marginal returns. When a top-tier account's ROI begins declining, adding budget often yields less benefit than shifting some budget to promising middle-tier accounts. Middle-tier accounts need stable testing budgets for optimization, recommended at 20%-30% of total budget. Bottom-tier accounts should have budget caps to prevent sustained losses from eroding team profits.</p>
<p>A reference ratio is: Top-tier accounts 50%-60%, middle-tier accounts 25%-35%, bottom-tier accounts 10%-15%. However, specific ratios should adjust based on team business objectives and risk tolerance.</p>
<h2>How to Establish Dynamic Tier Adjustment Mechanisms</h2>
<p>Account tiers aren't static. Establish fixed review cycles, such as weekly or bi-weekly tier evaluations.</p>
<p>During evaluation, focus on key metrics: ROI trends over the past 14 days, conversion volume fluctuations, and overall account spend changes. If a middle-tier account consistently outperforms the top-tier account average ROI for two consecutive weeks, consider upgrading it to top-tier. Conversely, if a top-tier account falls below the team baseline ROI for two consecutive weeks, investigate whether it's a market factor or an account-specific issue.</p>
<p>Tier adjustments should be quick, but budget adjustments should be slow. Upgrading can be fast, while downgrading requires caution because frequent budget changes affect system learning.</p>
<h2>Common Pitfalls in Account Tier Management</h2>
<p>The first pitfall is <strong>abandoning bottom-tier accounts after tiering</strong>. Bottom-tier accounts are often testing or newly created accounts; completely abandoning them means the team loses opportunities to explore new growth avenues. Each bottom-tier account should have clear testing objectives and time cycles.</p>
<p>The second pitfall is <strong>focusing solely on short-term ROI</strong>. Some teams downgrade or even pause accounts due to low weekly ROI, but in reality, those accounts might be accumulating long-tail conversions or providing data support for other accounts.</p>
<p>The third pitfall is <strong>overly complex tiering criteria</strong>. If the tiering logic requires operators to spend significant time calculating and maintaining it, the mechanism becomes difficult to sustain. A simplified approach is to set key metric thresholds, allowing the system to automatically flag account tier changes.</p>
